}\\right)^k \\cdot x^{-2k}\n$$\n\nNow combine the powers of $ x $:\n\n$$\nx^{3(5-k)} \\cdot x^{-2k} = x^{15 - 3k - 2k} = x^{15 - 5k}\n$$\n\nSo the term becomes:\n\n$$\nT_k = \\binom{5}{k} \\cdot 10^{5-k} \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^k \\cdot x^{15 - 5k}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find the Constant Term\n\nWe want the **constant term**, which means the exponent of $ x $ must be 0:\n\n$$\n15 - 5k = 0 \\Rightarrow k = 3\n$$\n\nSo the constant term is when $ k = 3 $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Plug in $ k = 3 $\n\n$$\nT_3 = \\binom{5}{3} \\cdot 10^{5-3} \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3\n$$\n\n$$\n= \\binom{5}{3} \\cdot 10^2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n= 10 \\cdot 100 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n= 1000 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) = -125\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{-125}\n$$", "diagnostic_seconds": 15.867242424748838}
